                <h2>Why Your Ad Account Reconciliation Always Has Issues</h2>
<p>Overseas advertising teams often encounter this situation: the charged amount doesn't match actual spend, the platform-issued invoice differs from backend data, or financial closing reveals messy accounts at month-end. These problems usually stem from multiple payment channels, complex account hierarchies, and currency exchange rate fluctuations.</p>
<p>Here's a proven reconciliation process to help you achieve full-chain verification from charging to invoicing.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Charge Record Organization (Reconciliation Starting Point)</h2>
<h3>Materials to Collect</h3>
<ul>
<li>Screenshot or export files of charging details from each ad platform backend</li>
<li>Payment vouchers in your company's financial system (bank transfer records, credit card statements)</li>
<li>Charging confirmation from agents or channel partners</li>
</ul>
<h3>Verification Points</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Date Alignment</strong>: Ensure each charge date matches the payment date, accounting for timezone differences (GMT+8 vs UTC)</li>
<li><strong>Amount Verification</strong>: When converting paid RMB to displayed USD/EUR, is the correct exchange rate used?</li>
<li><strong>Account Attribution</strong>: Did the charge reach the correct account (main/sub/agent account)?</li>
<li><strong>Balance Change Records</strong>: Does the post-charge account balance match the charged amount, excluding consumed portions?</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist Item</strong>: Does your charge record table include these seven fields: charge time, amount, currency, exchange rate, arrival account, consumed amount, remaining balance?</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Spend Data Verification (Core Process)</h2>
<h3>Data Source Comparison</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Data Source</th>
<th>Content</th>
<th>Common Issues</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Platform backend reports</td>
<td>Campaign/Ad Set/Ad-level spend</td>
<td>Timezone settings causing date differences</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account-level billing</td>
<td>Monthly aggregated spend</td>
<td>Estimated vs. final values</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>API exported data</td>
<td>Programmatically obtained spend</td>
<td>Delays due to refresh frequency</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Verification Points</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Date Range Confirmation</strong>: Does the reconciliation period cover the complete calendar month, excluding cross-month spend?</li>
<li><strong>Spend Attribution Standardization</strong>: Using platform report "end date" or "spend date"? The entire team needs consistent standards</li>
<li><strong>Anomalous Spend Flagging</strong>: Single-day spend exceeding 200% of average requires separate notation</li>
<li><strong>Refund and Credit Processing</strong>: Platforms sometimes refund spend (e.g., invalid traffic refunds), confirm if these have been credited</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist Item</strong>: Is the variance between platform report spend and billing spend within ±2%? Exceeding this range requires investigation.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Invoice Validation (Financial Compliance)</h2>
<h3>Invoice Acquisition by Platform</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Meta (Facebook)</strong>: Download monthly invoices from "Billing &gt; Payment Settings," distinguish between "Commercial Invoice" and "Receipt"</li>
<li><strong>Google Ads</strong>: Export through "Tools &gt; Billing &gt; Transactions," invoice amounts based on "Billing Account" not individual accounts</li>
<li><strong>TikTok</strong>: Obtain from "Assets &gt; Billing Center," confirm whether it's a "spend tax invoice" or "prepaid charge invoice"</li>
<li><strong>Other platforms</strong>: Some channel partners provide invoicing services, requiring additional verification of invoicing entity relationship to charged entity</li>
</ul>
<h3>Verification Points</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Amount Matching</strong>: Does the invoice amount match the spend amount? Watch for platform service fees or tax differences</li>
<li><strong>Company Information Verification</strong>: Does the invoice name, tax ID, and company address match the business license?</li>
<li><strong>Currency and Exchange Rate</strong>: Does the invoice currency match the contract agreement? Multi-currency situations require retention of exchange rate calculation basis</li>
<li><strong>Invoice Timeliness</strong>: Some platform invoices take 3-5 business days after the billing cycle ends; finance needs to account for wait time</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist Item</strong>: Is the invoice within the tax filing validity period? Recommended to complete all invoice archiving within 15 days after the billing cycle ends.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Variance Tracking and Resolution (Closed-Loop Management)</h2>
<h3>Common Variance Reasons</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Variance Type</th>
<th>Possible Cause</th>
<th>Resolution</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Charge not received</td>
<td>Payment channel delay/failure</td>
<td>Contact agent or platform support, preserve communication records</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spend exceeds charge</td>
<td>Sub-account shared quota/pre-authorized spend</td>
<td>Check account structure, limit sub-account spending caps</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Invoice amount low</td>
<td>Platform credits/rebates not recorded</td>
<td>Confirm rebate agreement, determine if supplementary invoice needed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Exchange rate variance</td>
<td>Different rates used for charge vs. spend</td>
<td>Establish rate locking mechanism, document calculation standards</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Resolution Process</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Variance Recording</strong>: Log each variance item in the "Ad Account Variance Tracking Sheet"</li>
<li><strong>Cause Tracing</strong>: Contact platform or agent for variance explanation, confirm via email</li>
<li><strong>Adjustment Confirmation</strong>: After confirming adjustment amount, obtain platform-stamped adjustment letter or supplementary invoice</li>
<li><strong>Archive for Reference</strong>: Archive reconciliation results, variance explanations, and communication records for at least 12 months</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist Item</strong>: Are there any unresolved variance items this month? If yes, note the responsible person and planned resolution date.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Monthly Reconciliation Complete Checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] All charge records exported and verified against payment vouchers</li>
<li>[ ] Platform report spend and account billing variance within acceptable range</li>
<li>[ ] Invoices obtained and amounts match spend data</li>
<li>[ ] Exchange rate calculation basis documented and archived</li>
<li>[ ] All variance items located with causes identified and resolution plans established</li>
<li>[ ] Reconciliation results shared with finance lead for confirmation and sign-off</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p><strong>Additional Recommendations</strong>: If your team manages multiple ad platforms and accounts, consider using a unified financial reconciliation template or ERP system integration to avoid manual aggregation errors. Additionally, setting up charge alerts (automatic reminders when balance falls below a threshold) can reduce delivery interruptions due to insufficient balance at the source.</p>
